Subject: Frees the `nip_catch` pop table on the success path of `js_create_function`

What this does
  Frees the `nip_catch` pop table on the success path of `js_create_function`.

Why we need it
  `js_create_function` has two exits: the fail path calls `js_free_function_def`, which frees `fd->regvm_nip_pop`, but the success path frees only `fd->regvm_code` (once copied into the bytecode object) and then drops `fd` with `free_objlit_sites` + `js_free`, so the `uint16_t[bc_len]` table `compute_stack_size` allocates whenever an `OP_nip_catch` pops a count other than 2 was leaked. The table is read only while compiling, so freeing it on the success path is safe.

What it adds
  `js_free(ctx, fd->regvm_nip_pop)` before `free_objlit_sites(ctx, fd)` in the success path of `js_create_function`, under `QJS_REGVM`.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
diff --git a/quickjs.c b/quickjs.c
index 65747d2..5cbb959 100644
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -64479,6 +64479,12 @@ static JSValue js_create_function(JSContext *ctx, JSFunctionDef *fd)
         list_del(&fd->link);
     }
 
+#if QJS_REGVM
+    /* fd->regvm_code was already freed once copied into b; the nip_catch pop
+       table has no other owner. */
+    js_free(ctx, fd->regvm_nip_pop);
+    fd->regvm_nip_pop = NULL;
+#endif
     free_objlit_sites(ctx, fd);
     js_free(ctx, fd);
     return JS_MKPTR(JS_TAG_FUNCTION_BYTECODE, b);
